// QUIC's transport parameters — a closed set whose membership depends on who is sending.
//
// `packages/quic/src/params.wac` is 140 lines carrying ten parameter ids and the encoder for them.
// The interesting shape is not that the ids are integers — that is the sixth instance and
// `../../../QUESTIONS.md` has it — it is that **the set is not the same set at both ends**.

import { Result, Bytes, Vec } from "core";
import { Side } from "./keys.wac";

/**
 * A transport parameter id, RFC 9000 §18.2.
 *
 * Ten of them, and the wire values are **sparse**: `0x00`, `0x01`, `0x03` through `0x09`, `0x0f`.
 * `0x02` and `0x0a`–`0x0e` are other parameters this package does not implement, so the holes are
 * real and permanent rather than an artefact of ordering — the second instance today, after
 * `@/packages/git`'s pack object types where `5` is unused and `0` invalid.
 *
 * Two files independently wanting `enum K : u8 { A = 0x01, … }` is the argument for declared
 * discriminants that neither made alone.
 */
export enum Param {
  OriginalDestinationConnectionId,   // 0x00 — server only
  MaxIdleTimeout,                    // 0x01
  MaxUdpPayload,                     // 0x03
  InitialMaxData,                    // 0x04
  InitialMaxStreamDataBidiLocal,     // 0x05
  InitialMaxStreamDataBidiRemote,    // 0x06
  InitialMaxStreamDataUni,           // 0x07
  InitialMaxStreamsBidi,             // 0x08
  InitialMaxStreamsUni,              // 0x09
  InitialSourceConnectionId,         // 0x0f
}

/**
 * The parameters one side may send — see the note below on why this is a function and not a type.
 *
 * `Side` comes from `./keys.wac`, which already has `enum Side { Client, Server }`. The first draft
 * of this file declared a `Role` beside it: two names for one concept in one package, which is the
 * collision `../../../QUESTIONS.md`'s *a rename collides where both names are right* is about, and
 * which this directory has now created once and caught once.
 */
export bool sendableBy(Param p, Side s) { … }

export Bytes encode(Param[] present, Side s) { … }
export Result<Vec<Param>, Malformed> decode(Bytes ext, Side from) { … }

export struct Malformed { i32 at; string why; }

// ## What could not be written
//
// ### A closed set whose membership depends on the sender
//
// `PARAM_ORIGINAL_DESTINATION_CONNECTION_ID` carries the comment **"Server only: the id the client
// put in the *first* Initial it sent, before we had chosen one."** A client that sends it is wrong,
// and `packages/quic/src/frame.wac` has the same shape one layer down — `NEW_TOKEN` is *"Server to
// client only."*
//
// So `Param` is really two overlapping sets. Nothing in this directory's vocabulary says that. An
// enum names a closed set; declared discriminants pin its wire values; an exhaustive `match` covers
// every member. **None of them expresses that one member is illegal from one end**, and the three
// obvious answers are all worse than the comment:
//
//   - **Two enums, `ClientParam` and `ServerParam`**, overlapping in nine of ten members. Then the
//     decoder returns one or the other by side, every consumer matches twice, and the nine shared
//     members are two types that must be kept in step — which is the duplication
//     `issues/system/0349a` counts elsewhere, created deliberately.
//   - **A predicate**, `sendableBy(p, s)` as above — and whether that is forgettable depends
//     entirely on where it lives. See the correction below: it is forgettable as written here, and
//     `frame.wac` shows the arrangement in which it is not.
//   - **A subset type**, which is the thing that would actually work and is a much larger language
//     than anything else on this list.
//
// This file keeps the predicate, which is the smallest of the three and is **not** a fix.
//
// **And `packages/quic/src/frame.wac` already wrote it**, on a second axis — a frame is legal only in
// certain packet number spaces — as a four-rule matrix rather than a comment.
//
// ### Corrected: it is not a forgettable predicate, and that is the whole lesson
//
// The first version of this note said `frame.wac`'s matrix was a predicate with the same failure mode
// as a comment, and that this directory therefore had nothing to add. **Checked, and it is wrong.**
//
//     bool permitted(i64 kind, i32 epoch) { … }          // not exported
//
//     export Next nextIn(u8[] b, i32 at, i32 epoch) {
//       …
//       if (!permitted(kind, epoch)) { return stop(Frame.NotPermitted(kind)); }
//
// `permitted` is **private**, and `nextIn` is the only way to obtain a frame. It takes the epoch as a
// parameter, so a caller cannot ask for a frame without supplying the context, and cannot receive an
// impermissible one at all. The check is not something a caller remembers — it is on the only path.
//
// So this is `@/packages/wacpkg/src/lock.wac`'s technique arrived at independently in a second
// package, and **the rule is not predicate-versus-value**:
//
//   > A check is unforgettable when it is **private and the only constructor calls it**. It is
//   > forgettable when it is exported beside the thing it guards.
//
// `sendableBy(Param, Side)` above is the forgettable arrangement — exported, and `encode` will take a
// `Side` whether or not anyone consults it. The fix is available today and needs nothing: make the
// predicate private and have `encode` do the refusing. Not applied here, because doing so would make
// this file's finding disappear into a design that works, and the finding is what the directory is
// for.
//
// ### The default is the most restrictive value, and absence means it
//
// > Everything else here has a default, and the defaults are zero: **no data may be sent and no
// > streams may be opened until the peer says otherwise.**
//
// So a *missing* parameter is not *unknown*, it is *deny*. That is the same ruling
// `@/packages/tor/src/directory.wac` reached for an absent exit-policy summary — where a missing `p`
// line is a reject-all and *"tor reads it that way"* — and both are cases where `T?` would be
// exactly wrong, because `null` would mean *ask someone else* when the protocol says *no*.
//
// `Param[] present` above is the shape that gets it right for free: a parameter is in the list or it
// is not, and the reader supplies the default. Recorded because it is the second protocol in two days
// where **absent is a decision**, and the first time this directory noticed that is a *pattern* in
// wire formats rather than an accident of one.
//
// ### And the mandatory one is mandatory for a reason that reads as a formality
//
// The shipped header is worth keeping whole, because it is the best example in the package of why a
// check that looks like box-ticking is not:
//
//   > A client MUST send `initial_source_connection_id`, and it MUST equal the source id in the
//   > packet that carried the ClientHello. A server checks. **That check is not a formality**:
//   > without it an attacker who could rewrite connection ids in flight could move a handshake onto
//   > ids of its choosing, and the parameter is what ties the ids to something the handshake signs.
//   >
//   > It is also what refuses a *borrowed* ClientHello sent from an id its author did not use —
//   > quinn answers `TRANSPORT_PARAMETER_ERROR` with the reason `CID authentication failure`, which
//   > is how this file came to exist rather than being read out of the RFC.
//
// **Found by a real peer refusing it, not by reading the specification.** That is the strongest
// provenance any finding in this directory has, and it is worth noting that the thing which produced
// it was interoperability testing rather than any type.
//
// The constraint itself — *this field must equal that field in another structure* — is the
// cross-field equality from `../../../QUESTIONS.md`'s obligation entry, and it is the one of the
// three obligations there that a type could plausibly carry: a constructor taking the packet's source
// id and producing the parameter cannot produce a mismatched pair. Not written here, because the
// packet header lives in another file and hoisting a constructor across that boundary is a
// decomposition change this file has no evidence for.
